            "heading": "7. Summary",
            "html": "<div class=\"detail-table-wrap\"><table class=\"detail-table\"><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Core Point</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The Father</td><td>Unbegotten, source and fountain of the Trinity in eternal relations</td></tr><tr><td>Eternal generation</td><td>Son is eternally begotten — not created, not in time, of the same essence as the Father</td></tr><tr><td>Incarnation / Chalcedon</td><td>One person, two natures: without confusion, change, division, or separation</td></tr><tr><td>Role vs. essence</td><td>Son submits to the Father in role and in the economy of redemption; equal in essence</td></tr><tr><td>Economic vs. ontological Trinity</td><td>God in history reflects God as He eternally is; the economic reveals the ontological</td></tr><tr><td>John 5, 10, 17</td><td>Real distinction of persons; full equality of essence and honour</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
            "plainText": "| Concept | Core Point |\n|---------|-----------|\n| The Father | Unbegotten, source and fountain of the Trinity in eternal relations |\n| Eternal generation | Son is eternally begotten — not created, not in time, of the same essence as the Father |\n| Incarnation / Chalcedon | One person, two natures: without confusion, change, division, or separation |\n| Role vs. essence | Son submits to the Father in role and in the economy of redemption; equal in essence |\n| Economic vs. ontological Trinity | God in history reflects God as He eternally is; the economic reveals the ontological |\n| John 5, 10, 17 | Real distinction of persons; full equality of essence and honour |"
